  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1052 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nA relatively dreary overnight period is currently progressing\nacross the Mid-South with some light showers continuing to move\nthrough the region. This light, shower activity will continue to\npush northward through the overnight hours with mostly dry\nconditions by Monday afternoon. Ample cloud coverage is expected\nto remain with WAA filtering into the area. Highmainly \ntemperatures will be a bit warmer though on Monday, with highs \nreturning to the 70s across the area.\n\nInto Tuesday, temperatures will surge even further in front of an\napproaching frontal boundary with highs reaching the upper 70s to\nnear 80 degrees. The aforementioned frontal boundary will be\nassociated with an upper-level trough axis that will be moving\noff the Rockies and through the Plains. The main low pressure\nsystem is expected to pull northward through the daytime on\nTuesday. A line of showers and thunderstorms will be ongoing to\nour west, with some question as to the strength and severity as\nit arrives into our area Tuesday afternoon and evening. CAM\nguidance is slowly coming into range, painting a picture of ample\nshear but potentially lacking lapse rates and surface CAPE. While\nCAPE thresholds can generally be a bit lower in our cool-season\nsevere weather scenarios, MUCAPE values are still certainly\nlacking for there to be a lot of confidence in a severe weather\nset-up. The majority of the CAM guidance does have surface\ninstability becoming shunted as the line of thunderstorms arrives\nin our area. Nonetheless, if storms were able to tap into this\npotential and maintain strength as they move into the area, a\nstrong thunderstorm or two capable of producing damaging winds\ngusts or a tornado cannot be ruled out. The overall chance of\nthis does remain fairly low, with LREF guidance of > 250 J/kg of\nCAPE and 30 kts of shear generally in the 15 to 20% range west of\nthe Mississippi River and into a small portion of northwest MS.\nHREF is slowly coming into range and is currently portraying an \neven more unfavorable environment. All in all, this will be \nsomething to watch over the next 48 hours, but some more favorable\nconditions would need to develop for confidence in severe weather\nto increase. What guidance does agree on is somewhat anonymously \nhigh PWAT values for much of the area, with values over the 97th \npercentile. Right now it looks like much of the area will receive \nbetween an inch to two inches of precipitation, with a medium \nchance (40 to 60%) chance of some locations generally south of \nI-40 exceeding the two inch mark. While this should not create \nsignificant flooding issues, locally heavy downpours could result \nin ponding and localized flooding in flood-prone areas.\n\nGuidance has come into better agreement with pushing the midweek\nsystem out of our area relatively quickly on Wednesday, although\nslightly above-normal temperatures may persist into Thursday\ndespite the boundary. By Friday, a series of dry, reinforcing\nfrontal boundaries will push through the area and finally knock\nour temperatures back down to near normal. Latest 6 to 10 day\nguidance from CPC does keep the region in favorable, near-normal\ntemperatures and below-normal precipitation into next weekend.\nWith this in mind, next weekend looks pretty great with high\ntemperatures spanning the 60s and overnight lows in the 30s to\n40s.",
